leftmenu"><a href="/php-gmp_legendre-function">PHP gmp_legendre() Function</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-gmp_mod-function">PHP gmp_mod() Function</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-gmp_prob_prime-function">PHP gmp_prob_prime() function</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-gmp_random_bits-function">PHP gmp_random_bits() function</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-gmp_random_range-function">PHP gmp_random_range() function</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-gmp_root-function">PHP gmp_root() function</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-gmp_rootrem-function">PHP gmp_rootrem() function</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-gmp_random_seed-function">PHP gmp_random_seed() function</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-gmp_scan0-function">PHP gmp_scan0() function</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-gmp_scan1-function">PHP gmp_scan1() function</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-gmp_setbit-function">PHP gmp_setbit() function</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-gmp-gmp_testbit-function">PHP GMP gmp_testbit() Function</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-gmp_random-function">PHP gmp_random() function</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-array-chunk-function">PHP Array Chunk() Function</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-gmp_xor-function">PHP gmp_xor() function</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-to-create-html-table-with-a-while-loop-in-php">How to create html table with a while loop in PHP</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-to-encrypt-or-decrypt-a-string-in-php">How to Encrypt or Decrypt a String in PHP</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-output-buffering-in-php">What is Output Buffering in PHP</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/codeslide-php-script">CodeSlide PHP Script</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-login-form">PHP Login Form</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/sticky-form-in-php">Sticky Form in PHP</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-frameworks">PHP Frameworks</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-versions">PHP Versions</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-database-connection">PHP Database Connection</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-syntax">PHP Syntax</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/difference-between-session-and-cookies-in-php">Difference between Session and Cookies in PHP</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-anonymous-functions">PHP Anonymous Functions</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/php-built-in-functions">PHP Built-in Functions</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/advantages-and-disadvantages-of-php">Advantages and Disadvantages of PHP</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/crud-operations-in-php-and-mysql">CRUD Operations in PHP and MySQL</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/ajax-integration-in-php">AJAX integration in PHP</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-to-run-a-php-file-using-visual-studio-code">How to run a PHP file using Visual Studio Code</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-to-connect-php-to-mysql">How to connect PHP to MySQL</a></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="col-12 col-md-7"><div class="overview"><div id="city"><table><tbody><tr><td><div id="bottomnextup"><a class="next" href="php-tutorial">next →</a> <a class="next" href="php-tutorial">← prev</a></div><h1 class="h1">PHP MCQ</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>21 Jun 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 36 min read</span></div><h2 class="h2">PHP Basics &amp; Fundamentals</h2><p><strong>1. PHP stands for -</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>Hypertext Preprocessor</li><li>Pretext Hypertext Preprocessor</li><li>Personal Home Processor</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(1)"><span id="btntext1">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(1)"><span id="btntext1">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace1"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer1"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) Hypertext Preprocessor</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> PHP stands for Hypertext Preprocessor. <a href="php-tutorial">PHP</a> is an open-source, interpreted, and object-oriented scripting language that can be executed on the server-side. It is well suited for web development.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>2. Who is known as the father of PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>Drek Kolkevi</li><li>List Barely</li><li>Rasmus Lerdrof</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(2)"><span id="btntext2">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(2)"><span id="btntext2">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace2"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer2"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) Rasmus Lerdrof</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> PHP was created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 but appeared in the market in 1995. PHP 7.4.0 is the latest version of PHP, which was released on 28 November.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>3. Which of the following is the default file extension of PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>.php</li><li>.hphp</li><li>.xml</li><li>.html</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(3)"><span id="btntext3">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(3)"><span id="btntext3">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace3"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer3"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) .php</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> Generally, a PHP file contains HTML tags and some PHP scripting code. It's very easy to create a simple PHP example. The programs in PHP are saved with the .php extension.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>4. Which of the following is correct to add a comment in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>&amp; …… &amp;</li><li>// ……</li><li>/* …… */</li><li>Both (B) and (C)</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(4)"><span id="btntext4">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(4)"><span id="btntext4">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace4"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer4"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) Both (B) and (C)</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> <a href="php-comments">PHP comments</a> can be used to describe any line of code so that other developers can understand the code easily. PHP supports single-line and multi-line comments. There are two ways to use single-line comments in PHP: // (C++ style single-line comment) and # (Unix Shell style single-line comment). For multiple lines comment in PHP, you need to enclose all lines within /* …..*/.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>5. What does PEAR stand for?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>PHP extension and application repository</li><li>PHP enhancement and application reduce</li><li>PHP event and application repository</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(5)"><span id="btntext5">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(5)"><span id="btntext5">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace5"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer5"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) PHP extension and application repository</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> <a href="php-pear">PEAR</a> is a framework and repository for reusable PHP components. PEAR stands for PHP Extension and Application Repository. It contains all types of <a href="run-php-code-in-xampp">PHP code</a> snippets and libraries.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>6. Which of the following functions displays information about PHP and its configuration?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>A) php_info()</li><li>B) phpinfo()</li><li>C) info()</li><li>D) None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(6)"><span id="btntext6">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(6)"><span id="btntext6">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace6"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer6"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) phpinfo()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The phpinfo() function provides information about the PHP configuration and installation in your system. The phpinfo() function is generally used to check the configuration settings.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>7. Which of the following is/are the code editors in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>A) Notepad++</li><li>B) Notepad</li><li>C) Adobe Dreamweaver</li><li>D) All of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(7)"><span id="btntext7">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(7)"><span id="btntext7">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace7"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer7"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) All of the above</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> PHP programs can be written on any editor, such as Notepad, Notepad++, Dreamweaver, etc. These programs save with a .php extension, i.e., filename.php, inside the htdocs folder.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>8. Which of the following is used to end a statement in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>. (dot)</li><li>; (semicolon)</li><li>! (exclamation)</li><li>/ (slash)</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(8)"><span id="btntext8">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(8)"><span id="btntext8">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace8"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer8"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) ; (semicolon)</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> PHP statements end with a semicolon (;).</p></div><hr/><p><strong>9. What does SPL stand for in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>Standard PHP Library</li><li>Simple PHP Library</li><li>Simple PHP List</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(9)"><span id="btntext9">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(9)"><span id="btntext9">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace9"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer9"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) Standard PHP Library</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> In PHP, SPL stands for "Standard PHP Library."</p></div><hr/><h2 class="h2">Variables &amp; Data Types</h2><p><strong>10. Variable name in PHP starts with -</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>! (Exclamation)</li><li>$ (Dollar)</li><li>&amp; (AmpersanD)</li><li># (Hash)</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(10)"><span id="btntext10">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(10)"><span id="btntext10">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace10"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer5"><p><strong>Answer:</strong>(b) $ (Dollar)</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> In PHP, a variable is declared using a $ (Dollar) sign followed by the variable name. As PHP is a loosely typed language, you don't need to declare the data types of variables. It automatically analyzes the values and makes conversions to the correct datatype. <a href="php-variables">Variables</a> in PHP are case-sensitive; for instance, the variables $name and $NAME are both treated as different variables.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>11. Which of the following is not a variable scope in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>Extern</li><li>Local</li><li>Static</li><li>Global</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(11)"><span id="btntext11">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(11)"><span id="btntext11">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace11"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer11"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) Extern</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The scope of a variable is defined as its range in the program under which it can be accessed. PHP has three types of variable scopes: Local variable, Global variable, and Static variable.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>12. Which of the following starts with __ (double underscore) in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>Inbuilt constants</li><li>User-defined constants</li><li>Magic constants</li><li>Default constants</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(12)"><span id="btntext12">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(12)"><span id="btntext12">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace12"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer12"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) $variable_name = value;</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> Magic constants are predefined constants in PHP that change based on their use. They start with a double underscore (__) and end with a double underscore. They are similar to other predefined constants, but as they change their values with the context, they are called magic constants.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>13. Which of the following is the correct way of defining a variable in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>$variable name = value;</li><li>$variable_name = value;</li><li>$variable_name = value</li><li>$variable name as value;</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(13)"><span id="btntext13">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(13)"><span id="btntext13">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace13"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer13"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) $variable_name = value;</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> In PHP, a variable is declared using a $ sign followed by the variable name. A PHP variable name cannot contain spaces. The syntax for declaring a variable in PHP is: $variable_name=value;</p></div><hr/><p><strong>14. What is the use of isset() function in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>The isset() function is used to check whether a variable is set or not</li><li>The isset() function is used to check whether the variable is free or not</li><li>The isset() function is used to check whether the variable is a string or not</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(14)"><span id="btntext14">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(14)"><span id="btntext14">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace14"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer14"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) The isset() function is used to check whether a variable is set or not</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The isset() function is a built-in function of PHP used to determine if a variable is set or not. If a variable is considered set, it means the variable is declared and has a different value from NULL. In short, it checks that the variable is declared and not null. This function returns true if the variable is not null; otherwise, it returns false.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>15. Which of the following functions is used to unset a variable in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>delete()</li><li>unset()</li><li>unlink()</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(15)"><span id="btntext15">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(15)"><span id="btntext15">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace15"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer15"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) unset()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The unset() function is a predefined variable handling function of PHP, which is used to unset a specified variable. In other words, "the unset() function destroys the variables."</p></div><hr/><p><strong>16. Which of the following functions in PHP can be used to test the type of any variable?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>showtype()</li><li>gettype()</li><li>settype()</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(16)"><span id="btntext16">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(16)"><span id="btntext16">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace16"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer16"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) gettype()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The gettype() function is an inbuilt function in PHP, and as its name implies, it returns the type of a variable. The gettype() function in PHP is used to check the type of an existing variable.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>17. String values in PHP must be enclosed within -</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>Double Quotes</li><li>Single Quotes</li><li>Both (A) and (B)</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(17)"><span id="btntext17">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(17)"><span id="btntext17">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace17"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer17"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) Both (A) and (B)</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> There are 4 ways to specify a string literal in PHP: single-quoted, double-quoted, heredoc syntax, and nowdoc syntax (since PHP 5.3). You can create a string in PHP by enclosing the text in single quotes. This is the easiest way to specify a string in PHP. In PHP, you can also specify a string by enclosing text within double quotes. However, escape sequences and variables will be interpreted when using double-quoted <a href="php-string">PHP strings</a>.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>18. Which of the following variable name is invalid?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>$newVar</li><li>$new_Var</li><li>$new-var</li><li>All of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(18)"><span id="btntext18">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(18)"><span id="btntext18">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace18"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer18"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) $new-var</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> In PHP, a variable is declared using a $ sign followed by the variable name. A variable name must start with a letter or an underscore (_) character. A PHP variable name cannot contain spaces or hyphens (-).</p></div><hr/><h2 class="h2">Operators &amp; Expressions</h2><p><strong>19. Which of the following is used for concatenation in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>+ (plus)</li><li>* (Asterisk)</li><li>. (dot)</li><li>append()</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(19)"><span id="btntext19">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(19)"><span id="btntext19">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace19"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer19"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) . (dot)</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> In PHP, the . (dot) operator is used for concatenation. Suppose there are two variables $a and $b, the statement $a . $b will concatenate both $a and $b.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>20. Consider the following PHP code snippet. What will be its output?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$a = 10;
echo ++$a;
echo $a++;
echo $a;
echo ++$a;
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>11111213</li><li>11121213</li><li>11111212</li><li>11111112</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(20)"><span id="btntext20">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(20)"><span id="btntext20">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace20"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer20"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) 11111213</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> This program demonstrates the behaviour of pre-increment (++$A) and post-increment ($a++) operators. ++$a increments the variable first, then uses its value. $a++ uses the variable's current value first, then increments it.</p><ul class="points"><li>$a = 10;</li><li>echo ++$a; // $a becomes 11, then 11 is echoed. Output: 11. $a is now 11.</li><li>echo $a++; // 11 is echoed, then $a becomes 12. Output: 11. $a is now 12.</li><li>echo $a; // 12 is echoed. Output: 12. $a is now 12.</li><li>echo ++$a; // $a becomes 13, then 13 is echoed. Output: 13. $a is now 13. Concatenated output: 11111213.</li></ul></div><hr/><p><strong>21. Examine the PHP code below. What output will it produce?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$a = 12;
--$a;
echo $a++;
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>11</li><li>12</li><li>10</li><li>error</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(21)"><span id="btntext21">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(21)"><span id="btntext21">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace21"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer21"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) 11</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The code demonstrates decrement and post-increment.</p><ul class="points"><li>$a = 12;</li><li>--$a; // $a is pre-decremented to 11. $a is now 11.</li><li>echo $a++; // The current value of $a (11) is echoed, then $a is post-incremented to 12. Output: 11. $a is now 12.</li></ul></div><hr/><p><strong>22. Given the following PHP code, predict its output.</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$x = "test";
$y = "this";
$z = "also";
$x .= $y .= $z ;
echo $x;
echo $y;
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>testthisthisalso</li><li>testthis</li><li>testthisalsothisalso</li><li>error at line 4</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(22)"><span id="btntext22">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(22)"><span id="btntext22">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace22"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer22"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) testthisalsothisalso</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> This example shows the right-to-left associativity of the combined assignment operator (.=) for string concatenation.</p><ul class="points"><li>$x = "test";, $y = "this";, $z = "also";</li><li>$y .= $z; is evaluated first: $y becomes "this" . "also", which is "thisalso".</li><li>Then $x .= $y; is evaluated: $x becomes "test" . "thisalso", which is "testthisalso".</li><li>echo $x; prints testthisalso.</li><li>echo $y; prints thisalso. Combined output: testthisalsothisalso.</li></ul></div><hr/><p><strong>23. Consider the PHP snippet provided. What will be the output?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$x = 1;
$y = 2;
if (++$x == $y++)
{
    echo "true ", $y, $x;
}
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>no output</li><li>true 32</li><li>true 23</li><li>true 22</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(23)"><span id="btntext23">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(23)"><span id="btntext23">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace23"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer23"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) true 32</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> Let's trace the execution:</p><ul class="points"><li>$x = 1;, $y = 2;</li><li>Inside if (++$x == $y++):</li><li>++$x: $x becomes 2. The value used in the comparison is 2.</li><li>$y++: The value used in comparison is 2. After comparison, $y becomes 3.</li><li>So, the condition becomes (2 == 2), which is true.</li><li>The if block executes: echo "true ", $y, $x;</li><li>$y is currently 3.</li><li>$x is currently 2. Output: true 32.</li></ul></div><hr/><p><strong>24. What will be the output of the following PHP script?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$y = 2;
$w = 4;
$y *= $w /= $y;
echo $y, $w;
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>80.5</li><li>44</li><li>82</li><li>42</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(24)"><span id="btntext24">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(24)"><span id="btntext24">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace24"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer24"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) 42</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The combined assignment operators (*=, /=) have right-to-left associativity.</p></div><ul class="points"><li>$y = 2;, $w = 4;</li><li>$w /= $y; is evaluated first: $w becomes 4 / 2, which is 2. $w is now 2.</li><li>Then $y *= $w; is evaluated: $y becomes $y * $w (which is 2 * 2), resulting in 4. $y is now 4.</li><li>echo $y, $w; prints the current values of $y and $w. Output: 42.</li></ul><hr/><p><strong>25. Determine the output of the PHP code snippet provided.</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$y = 2;
if ($y-- == ++$y)
{
    echo $y;
}
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>2</li><li>1</li><li>3</li><li>no output</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(25)"><span id="btntext25">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(25)"><span id="btntext25">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace25"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer25"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) 2</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> Let's trace the operations within the if condition:</p><ul class="points"><li>$y = 2;</li><li>if ($y-- == ++$y):</li><li>$y--: The value 2 is used for the comparison on the left side. After the comparison, $y becomes 1.</li><li>++$y: $y (which is now 1) is incremented to 2. The value 2 is used for the comparison on the right side.</li><li>The condition becomes (2 == 2), which is true.</li><li>The if block executes, and echo $y; prints the current value of $y. Output: 2.</li></ul></div><hr/><p><strong>26. What will be the result of executing the following PHP code?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$y = 2;
if (**$y == 4)
{
    echo $y;
}
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>4</li><li>2</li><li>error at line2</li><li>no output</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(26)"><span id="btntext26">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(26)"><span id="btntext26">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace26"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer26"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) error at line2</p><p>Description: The ** operator is used for exponentiation in some languages, but it is not a valid operator in standard PHP for numerical calculations or variable manipulation in this context. Attempting to use it as shown will result in a parse error.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>27. Consider the PHP code and determine its output.</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$y = 2;
if (--$y == 2 || $y xor --$y)
{
    echo $y;
}
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>1</li><li>0</li><li>2</li><li>no output</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(27)"><span id="btntext27">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(27)"><span id="btntext27">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace27"> </div><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) 0</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> Let's break down the if condition with short-circuiting and operator behaviour:</p><ul class="points"><li>$y = 2;</li><li>if (--$y == 2 || $y xor --$y):</li><li>--$y: $y becomes 1. The left side of == is 1.</li><li>1 == 2 evaluates to false.</li><li>Since the first part of || (OR) is false, the second part $y xor --$y is evaluated.</li><li>Inside $y xor --$y:</li><li>Left operand: $y is currently 1.</li><li>Right operand: --$y decrements $y to 0. The value used for this operand is 0.</li></ul><p>The xor (exclusive OR) operator returns true if exactly one of its operands is true. In a boolean context, 1 is true, and 0 is false. So, true xor false evaluates to true.</p><p>Since the overall if condition is true, the echo $y; statement executes.</p><p>At this point, $y has been decremented to 0. Output: 0.</p><hr/><p><strong>28. Analyze the following PHP code and determine its output. Consider operator precedence and evaluation order.</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="python" name="code">&lt;?php
$y = 2;
if (--$y &lt;&gt; ($y != $y++))
{
    echo $y;
}
?&gt;
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>1</li><li>0</li><li>2</li><li>undefined</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(28)"><span id="btntext28">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(28)"><span id="btntext28">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace28"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer28"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) undefined</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> This question involves multiple increment/decrement operators and comparisons within a single expression, leading to undefined behaviour due to the unspecified order of evaluation in PHP for certain complex expressions. While operator precedence and associativity define how parts of an expression are grouped, they do not dictate the order in which operands are evaluated. Different <a href="php-versions">PHP versions</a> or even different surrounding code contexts might yield varying results. Relying on such behaviour is discouraged. Thus, the output is considered undefined.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>29. What is the output of the given PHP code snippet?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$x = 0;
echo $x-- != ++$x;
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>1</li><li>0</li><li>error</li><li>no output</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(29)"><span id="btntext29">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(29)"><span id="btntext29">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace29"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer29"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) 0</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> Let's analyze the expression ($x-- != ++$x):</p><ul class="points"><li>$x is initialized to 0.</li><li>$x--: The value 0 is used for the left side of the != comparison. After this, $x becomes -1.</li><li>++$x: $x (which is now -1) is pre-incremented to 0. The value 0 is used for the right side of the != comparison.</li><li>The comparison becomes (0 != 0), which evaluates to false.</li><li>When a boolean false is echoed in PHP, it is converted to 0. Output: 0.</li></ul></div><hr/><h2 class="h2">Control Structures</h2><p><strong>30. What will be the output of the following program?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$a;
if ($A)
{
echo "hi";
}
else
{
echo "How are you";
}
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Hi How are you</li><li>How are you</li><li>Hi</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(30)"><span id="btntext30">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(30)"><span id="btntext30">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace30"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer30"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) How are you</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> In the given program, the variable $a is uninitialized. So, the if condition will evaluate to false (as uninitialized variables are treated as NULL, which is false in a boolean context), and the echo statement of the else block will be printed on the screen.</p></div><p><strong>31. What will be the output of the following program?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$a = 0;
while ($a++)
{
echo "$a";
}
echo $a;
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>0</li><li>1</li><li>01</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(31)"><span id="btntext31">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(31)"><span id="btntext31">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace31"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer31"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) 1</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> In the given program, there is a post-increment ($a++). So, first, the condition is checked: the value 0 for $a is used in the while condition. Since 0 evaluates to false, the loop does not execute. After the condition evaluation, $a is incremented to 1. Finally, echo $a; prints the value of $a, which is 1.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>32. What will be the output of the following program?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$x = 15;
$y = 20;
if($x &lt; ++$x || $y &lt; ++$y)
{
echo "Hello World";
}
else
{
echo "Hii everyone";
}
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Hii everyone</li><li>Hello World</li><li>Hello World Hii everyone</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(32)"><span id="btntext32">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(32)"><span id="btntext32">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace32"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer32"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) Hello World</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> In the condition ($x &lt; ++$x || $y &lt; ++$y), the left side $x &lt; ++$x is evaluated first. ++$x increments $x to 16, so the comparison becomes 15 &lt; 16, which is true. Due to short-circuit evaluation of the || (OR) operator, if the first operand is true, the second operand ($y &lt; ++$y) is not evaluated. Therefore, the if block executes, printing "Hello World".</p></div><hr/><p><strong>33. What will be the output of the following program?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$a = "1";
switch($A)
{
case 1:
echo "Hello";
case 2:
echo "World";
default:
echo "This is tpointtech.com";
}
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>HelloWorld</li><li>This is [suspicious link removed]</li><li>Hello</li><li>HelloWorldThis is [suspicious link removed]</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(33)"><span id="btntext33">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(33)"><span id="btntext33">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace33"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer33"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) HelloWorldThis is [suspicious link removed]</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> In the given program, there is no break statement provided for the case blocks, so all cases will get executed once a match is found. Given variable $a is initialized with the string "1". In PHP, switch performs a loose comparison by default, so "1" matches case 1. Since there's no break, execution "falls through" to case 2 and then to the default block.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>34. What will be the output of the following program?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
while()
{
echo "Hello World";
}
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Hello World</li><li>Infinite loop</li><li>no output</li><li>Error</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(34)"><span id="btntext34">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(34)"><span id="btntext34">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace34"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer34"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) Error</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The while loop is also called an Entry control loop because the condition is checked before entering the loop body. This means that first, the condition is evaluated. If the condition is true, the block of code will be executed. A while loop cannot be defined without a condition, so an empty condition while() results in a Parse error.</p></div><hr/><h2 class="h2">Functions</h2><p><strong>35. Which of the following is the correct way to create a function in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>Create myFunction()</li><li>New_function myFunction()</li><li>function myFunction()</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(35)"><span id="btntext35">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(35)"><span id="btntext35">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace35"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer35"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) function myFunction()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> You can declare and call user-defined functions easily. The syntax to declare user-defined functions is given below: function functionname(){ //code to be executed }</p></div><hr/><p><strong>36. Which of the following PHP functions is used to generate a unique ID?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>id()</li><li>mdid()</li><li>uniqid()</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(36)"><span id="btntext36">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(36)"><span id="btntext36">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace36"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer36"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) uniqid()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The uniqid() function is used to generate a unique ID based on the current time in microseconds, often useful for naming files or database entries where uniqueness is required. (Note: The original description for this answer was a copy-paste of a function declaration example, which has been corrected here.)</p></div><hr/><p><strong>37. What is the use of the sprintf() function in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>The sprintf() function is used to print the output of the program</li><li>The sprintf() function is used to send output to a variable</li><li>Both of the above</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(37)"><span id="btntext37">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(37)"><span id="btntext37">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace37"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer37"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) The sprintf() function is used to send output to a variable</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The sprintf() is an in-built function of PHP that writes a formatted string to a variable. It returns a formatted string. The sprintf() function is similar to the printf() function, but the only difference between them is that sprintf() saves the output into a string instead of displaying the formatted message on the browser like the printf() function.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>38. Consider the following PHP function definition and call. What will be the script's output?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
function calc($price, $tax="")
{
    $total = $price + ($price * $tax);
    echo "$total";
}
calc(42);
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Error</li><li>0</li><li>42</li><li>84</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(38)"><span id="btntex385">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(38)"><span id="btntext38">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace38"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer38"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) 42</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> In PHP, function parameters can have default values, making them optional. If $tax is not provided (as in calc(42)), it defaults to an empty string (""). When arithmetic operations are performed with a string, PHP attempts type juggling. An empty string "" is treated as 0 in a numeric context. So, $total = 42 + (42 * 0); which evaluates to $total = 42 + 0;, resulting in 42.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>39. Analyze the PHP code below, which defines a nested function. What output will it produce when executed?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;div class="codeblock"&gt;&lt;textarea name="code" class="xml"&gt; 
   
&lt;/textarea&gt;&lt;/div&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>I am b</li><li>I am bI am a</li><li>Error</li><li>I am a Error</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(39)"><span id="btntext39">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(39)"><span id="btntext39">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace39"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answe395"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) I am an Error</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> In PHP, a function defined inside another function is only declared (and thus callable) when the outer function is executed.</p><ul class="points"><li>The first call to a() executes:</li><li>function b() is defined and becomes available in the global scope.</li><li>echo 'I am a'; prints "I am a".</li><li>The second call to a() executes:</li><li>It attempts to redefine function b(). PHP does not allow the re-declaration of functions. This results in a fatal error: Fatal error: Cannot redeclare b(). Therefore, the output will be "I am a" followed by a fatal error message.</li></ul></div><hr/><p><strong>40. Given the PHP code with a nested function, predict the outcome.</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
function a()
{
    function b()
    {
        echo 'I am b';
    }
    echo 'I am a';
}
b();
a();
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>I am b</li><li>I am bI am a</li><li>Error</li><li>I am a Error</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(40)"><span id="btntext40">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(40)"><span id="btntext40">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace40"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer40"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) Error</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> In PHP, a function defined inside another function is only created and made available in the global scope when the outer function is called.</p><p>b(); is called first. At this point, function b() has not yet been defined because function a() has not been executed. This leads to a Fatal error: Call to undefined function b(). The script will terminate with an error before a() is ever called.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>41. Analyze the following PHP code regarding variable scope. What will be the output?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$op2 = "blabla";
function foo($op1)
{
    echo $op1;
    echo $op2;
}
foo("hello");
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>helloblabla</li><li>error</li><li>hello</li><li>helloblablablabla</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(41)"><span id="btntext41">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(41)"><span id="btntext41">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace41"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer41"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) hello</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> This example demonstrates PHP's default variable scope. Variables declared outside a function have global scope, but they are not automatically available inside functions. To access a global variable within a function, you must explicitly declare it with the global keyword or use the $_GLOBALS superglobal array. Without global $op2; inside foo(), $op2 inside the function is considered undefined.</p><ul class="points"><li>foo("hello"); is called.</li><li>echo $op1; prints "hello".</li><li>echo $op2; attempts to access $op2. Since $op2 was not passed as an argument and not declared global within the function, it is undefined within foo()'s local scope. PHP will typically issue a Notice: Undefined variable: op2 and treat $op2 as an empty string (which produces no visible output). Therefore, the visible output is "hello".</li></ul></div><hr/><p><strong>42. What is the result of executing the given PHP code?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
    function foo($msg)
    {
        echo "$msg";
    }
    $var1 = "foo";
    $var1("will this work");
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>error</li><li>$msg</li><li>0</li><li>will this work</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(42)"><span id="btntext42">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(42)"><span id="btntext42">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace42"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer42"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) will this work</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> PHP supports variable functions. This means that if a variable name has parentheses appended to it, PHP will look for a function with the same name as the variable's value and attempt to execute it.</p><ul class="points"><li>The function foo() is defined.</li><li>$var1 is assigned the string "foo".</li><li>$var1("will this work"); effectively calls foo("will this work");.</li><li>Inside foo(), echo "$msg"; prints the passed argument. Output: "will this work".</li></ul></div><hr/><p><strong>43. What character is produced by the following PHP code utilizing an ASCII function?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
   echo chr(52);
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>1</li><li>2</li><li>3</li><li>4</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(43)"><span id="btntext43">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(43)"><span id="btntext43">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace43"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer43"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) 4</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The chr() function returns the character corresponding to the given ASCII value. ASCII value 52 represents the character '4'. Therefore, the script will output '4'.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>44. What numeric value will be displayed by the following PHP code?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"> &lt;?php
    echo ord ("hi");
?&gt;
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>106</li><li>103</li><li>104</li><li>209</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(44)"><span id="btntext44">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(44)"><span id="btntext44">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace44"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer44"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) 104</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The ord() function returns the <a href="ascii-full-form">ASCII</a> value of the first character of a string. In the string "hi", the first character is 'h'. The ASCII value for 'h' is 104.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>45. Calculate the output of the following PHP mathematical function call.</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
    echo(atan(0.50));
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>0.11845976421345</li><li>0.23568451142521</li><li>0.46364760900081</li><li>1</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(45)"><span id="btntext45">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(45)"><span id="btntext45">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace45"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer45"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) 0.46364760900081</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The atan() function in PHP calculates the arc tangent (inverse tangent) of a given number, returning the result in radians. For atan(0.50), the approximate value is 0.46364760900081 radians.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>46. What will be printed by the PHP script that defines and accesses a constant?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
   define("GREETING","Hello you! How are you today?");
   echo constant("GREETING");
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Hello you! How are you today?</li><li>GREETING</li><li>GREETING, Hello you! How are you today?</li><li>"GREETING","Hello you! How are you today?"</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(46)"><span id="btntext46">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(46)"><span id="btntext46">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace46"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer46"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) Hello you! How are you today?</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The define() function is used to create a named constant in PHP. The constant() function then allows you to retrieve the value of a constant by its name, which is useful when the constant name is stored in a variable. In this case, the constant("GREETING") retrieves the value assigned to the constant "GREETING".</p></div><hr/><p><strong>47. What will be the output of the given PHP code snippet?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
    define("GREETING1","Hello you! How are you today?");
    define("GREETING2","Hello you! How are you today?");
    define("GREETING3","Hello you! How are you today?");
    echo defined("GREETING");
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>1</li><li>0</li><li>3</li><li>4</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(47)"><span id="btntext47">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(47)"><span id="btntext47">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace47"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer47"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) 0</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The defined() function checks whether a constant with a specified name has been defined. It returns true (which echoes as 1) if the constant exists and false (which echoes as 0) otherwise. Although GREETING1, GREETING2, and GREETING3 are defined, the code explicitly checks for a constant named "GREETING", which is not defined in this script. Therefore, defined("GREETING") evaluates to false, and echoing false results in 0.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>48. What will be the output of the following program?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$a = 15;
function show()
{
$a = 20;
echo "$a";
}
show();
echo "$a";
?&gt;
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>2015</li><li>2020</li><li>1515</li><li>0</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(48)"><span id="btntext48">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(48)"><span id="btntext48">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace48"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer48"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) 2015</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> In the given program, first, the show() function is called. Inside show(), a local variable $a is initialized to 20, and 20 is printed. This local $a is distinct from the global $a. Later, after the function call, the global variable $a (which retains its value of 15) is printed. So, the output of the above program will be 2015.</p></div><hr/><h2 class="h2">Arrays</h2><p><strong>49. Which of the following functions is used to sort an array in descending order?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>sort()</li><li>asrot()</li><li>dsort()</li><li>rsort()</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(49)"><span id="btntext49">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(49)"><span id="btntext49">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace49"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer49"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) rsort()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The PHP rsort() function is used to sort an indexed array in descending order. This function was introduced in PHP 4.0.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>50. Which of the following is the correct way to create an array in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>$season = array["summer" , "winter" , "spring" , "autumn"];</li><li>$season = array("summer" , "winter" , "spring" , "autumn");</li><li>$season = "summer" , "winter" , "spring" , "autumn";</li><li>All of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(50)"><span id="btntext50">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(50)"><span id="btntext50">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace50"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer50"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) $season = array("summer" , "winter" , "spring" , "autumn");</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The traditional way to create an array in PHP is using the array() construct. Since PHP 5.4, you can also use the shorthand square bracket syntax: $season = ["summer", "winter", "spring", "autumn"];.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>51. What will be the output of the following PHP code?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$cars = array("Volvo", "BMW", "Toyota");
echo "I like " . $cars[2] . ", " . $cars[1] . " and " . $cars[0] . ".";
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>I like Volvo, Toyota and BMW</li><li>I like Volvo, BMW and Toyota</li><li>I like BMW, Volvo and Toyota</li><li>I like Toyota, BMW and Volvo</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(51)"><span id="btntext51">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(51)"><span id="btntext51">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace51"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer51"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) I like Toyota, BMW and Volvo</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> This code demonstrates accessing array elements by their numerical index. PHP arrays are zero-indexed, meaning the first element is at index 0, the second at index 1, and so on. The echo statement concatenates strings and array elements using the . Operator based on the specified indices.</p><ul class="points"><li>$cars[2] refers to "Toyota".</li><li>$cars[1] refers to "BMW".</li><li>$cars[0] refers to "Volvo". The output will concatenate these in the order specified: "I like Toyota, BMW and Volvo."</li></ul></div><p><strong>52. Consider the PHP code snippet below. What output will print_r($C) produce?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$fname = array("Peter", "Ben", "Joe");
$age = array("35", "37", "43");
$c = array_combine($age, $fname);
print_r($C);
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Array (Peter Ben Joe)</li><li>Array ([Peter] =&gt; 35 [Ben] =&gt; 37 [Joe] =&gt; 43)</li><li>Array (35 37 43)</li><li>Array ([35] =&gt; Peter [37] =&gt; Ben [43] =&gt; Joe)</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(52)"><span id="btntext52">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(52)"><span id="btntext52">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace52"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer52"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) Array ([35] =&gt; Peter [37] =&gt; Ben [43] =&gt; Joe)</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The array_combine() function creates a new array by using the elements of one array as keys and the elements of another array as their corresponding values. In this case, $age provides the keys (35, 37, 43), and $fname provides the values (Peter, Ben, Joe). print_r() then displays the structure and content of the resulting array.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>53. What will be the output of the following PHP code?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$a=array("A","Cat","Dog","A","Dog");
$b=array("A","A","Cat","A","Tiger");
$c=array_combine($a,$B);
print_r(array_count_values($C));
?&gt;   
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Array ( [A] =&gt; 5 [Cat] =&gt; 2 [Dog] =&gt; 2 [Tiger] =&gt; 1 )</li><li>Array ( [A] =&gt; 2 [Cat] =&gt; 2 [Dog] =&gt; 1 [Tiger] =&gt; 1 )</li><li>Array ( [A] =&gt; 6 [Cat] =&gt; 1 [Dog] =&gt; 2 [Tiger] =&gt; 1 )</li><li>Array ( [A] =&gt; 2 [Tiger] =&gt; 1 )</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(53)"><span id="btntext53">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(53)"><span id="btntext53">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace53"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer53"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) Array ( [A] =&gt; 2 [Tiger] =&gt; 1 )</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> This code combines array_combine() with array_count_values().</p><p>$c = array_combine($a, $B);: When array_combine() is used, if there are duplicate keys in the first array ($A), the last occurrence of the key in $a will override previous ones.</p><p>"A" (first) =&gt; "A" (first)</p><p>"Cat" =&gt; "A" (seconD)</p><p>"Dog" (first) =&gt; "Cat"</p><p>"A" (seconD) =&gt; "A" (thirD) - This "A" key overwrites the first "A".</p><p>"Dog" (seconD) =&gt; "Tiger" - This "Dog" key overwrites the first "Dog". So, $c effectively becomes an Array ( [A] =&gt; A [Cat] =&gt; A [Dog] =&gt; Tiger ). (Wait, no. The keys are "A", "Cat", "Dog", "A", "Dog". The duplicates "A" and "Dog" as keys will be overwritten by their last occurrences. Correct $c from array_combine() with duplicate keys: The keys for $c are from $a. If $a has duplicate values, array_combine will only keep the last key-value pair for that specific key. $a = ["A","Cat","Dog","A","Dog"] $b = ["A","A","Cat","A","Tiger"] array_combine($a,$B) will result in: ["A" =&gt; "A"] (first "A" from $a, first "A" from $B) ["Cat" =&gt; "A"] ["Dog" =&gt; "Cat"] ["A" =&gt; "A"] (second "A" from $a, fourth "A" from $b - overwrites the first "A" entry) ["Dog" =&gt; "Tiger"] (second "Dog" from $a, fifth "Tiger" from $b - overwrites the first "Dog" entry) So, $c will be Array ( [A] =&gt; A, [Cat] =&gt; A, [Dog] =&gt; Tiger ).</p><p>print_r(array_count_values($C));: This function counts the occurrences of each value in the array $c.</p><p>"A" appears 2 times (from [A] =&gt; A and [Cat] =&gt; A).</p><p>"Tiger" appears 1 time (from [Dog] =&gt; Tiger). Therefore, the output is Array ( [A] =&gt; 2 [Tiger] =&gt; 1 ).</p></div><hr/><p><strong>54. Consider the following PHP code for array manipulation. What will print_r($result) output?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$a1 = array("a" =&gt; "red", "b" =&gt; "green", "c" =&gt; "blue", "d" =&gt; "yellow");
$a2 = array("e" =&gt; "red", "f" =&gt; "green", "g" =&gt; "blue", "h" =&gt; "orange");
$a3 = array("i" =&gt; "orange");
$a4 = array_merge($a2, $a3);
$result = array_diff($a1, $a4);
print_r($result);
?&gt;    
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Array ( [d] =&gt; yellow )</li><li>Array ( [i] =&gt; orange )</li><li>Array ( [h] =&gt; orange )</li><li>Array ( [d] =&gt; yellow [h] =&gt; orange )</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(54)"><span id="btntext54">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(54)"><span id="btntext54">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace54"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer54"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) Array ( [d] =&gt; yellow )</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> This code uses array_merge() and array_diff().</p><ul class="points"><li>$a4 = array_merge($a2, $a3);: This merges $a2 and $a3. Since keys are distinct, all elements are included. $a4 becomes Array ( [e] =&gt; red [f] =&gt; green [g] =&gt; blue [h] =&gt; orange [i] =&gt; orange ).</li><li>$result = array_diff($a1, $a4);: This function compares the values of $a1 against the values present in $a4. It returns an array containing the elements from $a1 whose values are not found in $a4.</li><li>$a1 values: "red", "green", "blue", "yellow"</li><li>$a4 values: "red", "green", "blue", "orange", "orange" "red", "green", and "blue" are present in both. "yellow" from $a1 is not present in $a4. Therefore, $result will contain only [d] =&gt; yellow.</li></ul></div><hr/><p><strong>55. Predict the output of the following PHP script, which involves array merging and combining.</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$a1 = array("red", "green");
$a2 = array("blue", "yellow");
$a3 = array_merge($a1, $a2);
$a4 = array("a", "b", "c", "d");
$a = array_combine($a4, $a3);
print_r($A);
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Array ( [a] =&gt; blue [b] =&gt; yellow [c] =&gt; red [d] =&gt; green )</li><li>Array ( [0] =&gt; blue [1] =&gt; yellow [2] =&gt; red [3] =&gt; green )</li><li>Array ( [0] =&gt; red [1] =&gt; green [2] =&gt; blue [3] =&gt; yellow )</li><li>Array ( [a] =&gt; red [b] =&gt; green [c] =&gt; blue [d] =&gt; yellow )</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(55)"><span id="btntext55">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(55)"><span id="btntext55">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace55"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer55"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) Array ( [a] =&gt; red [b] =&gt; green [c] =&gt; blue [d] =&gt; yellow )</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> This code demonstrates the combined use of array_merge() and array_combine().</p><ul class="points"><li>$a3 = array_merge($a1, $a2);: This merges the two indexed arrays. The numerical keys are re-indexed. $a3 becomes Array ( [0] =&gt; red [1] =&gt; green [2] =&gt; blue [3] =&gt; yellow ).</li><li>$a = array_combine($a4, $a3);: This uses $a4 as keys and $a3 as values.</li><li>Key "a" (from $a4) gets value "red" (from $a3[0]).</li><li>Key "b" (from $a4) gets value "green" (from $a3[1]).</li><li>Key "c" (from $a4) gets value "blue" (from $a3[2]).</li><li>Key "d" (from $a4) gets value "yellow" (from $a3[3]). The final $a array is Array ( [a] =&gt; red [b] =&gt; green [c] =&gt; blue [d] =&gt; yellow ).</li></ul></div><hr/><p><strong>56. What will be the output of the following PHP code?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$a = array("a" =&gt; "India", "b" =&gt; "Brazil", "c" =&gt; "China");
echo array_shift($A);
echo "&lt;br&gt;";
array_pop($A);
print_r($A);
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>India<br>Array ( [b] =&gt; brazil )</br></li><li>India<br>Array ( [a] =&gt; brazil )</br></li><li>China<br>Array ( [a] =&gt; India )</br></li><li>China<br/>Array ( [b] =&gt; brazil )</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(56)"><span id="btntext56">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(56)"><span id="btntext56">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace56"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer56"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a)<br/>India<br/>Array ( [b] =&gt; brazil )</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> This code uses array_shift() and array_pop().</p><ul class="points"><li>$a = array("a" =&gt; "india", "b" =&gt; "brazil", "c" =&gt; "china");</li><li>echo array_shift($A);: array_shift() removes the first element from the array ("a" =&gt; "India") and returns its value. It also re-indexes numerical keys if present.</li><li>"India" is echoed.</li><li>$a is now Array ( [b] =&gt; brazil [c] =&gt; china ).</li><li>echo "&lt;br&gt;";: Prints a line break.</li><li>array_pop($A);: array_pop() removes the last element from the array ("c" =&gt; "china") and returns its value (which is not caught here).</li><li>$a is now Array ( [b] =&gt; Brazil).</li><li>print_r($A);: Prints the remaining array. The combined output shows "India", followed by a line break, then Array ( [b] =&gt; Brazil).</li></ul></div><hr/><p><strong>57. What will be the output of the following PHP code?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$a1 = array_fill(1, 4, "hello");
$b1 = array_fill(5, 1, "php");
$a2 = array_merge($a1, $b1); // Corrected from $a2 = array_merge($a1, $a2);
print_r($a2);
echo "&lt;br&gt;";
print_r($b1);
?&gt;
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Array ( [1] =&gt; hello [4] =&gt; hello [5] =&gt; php )<br/>Array ( [5] =&gt; php )</li><li>Array ( [1] =&gt; hello [2] =&gt; hello [3] =&gt; hello [4] =&gt; hello )<br/>Array ( [5] =&gt; php )</li><li>Array ( [1] =&gt; hello [2] =&gt; hello [3] =&gt; hello [4] =&gt; hello [5] =&gt; php )<br/>Array ( [5] =&gt; php )</li><li>Array ( [1] =&gt; hello [2] =&gt; hello [3] =&gt; hello [4] =&gt; hello )<br/>Array ( [1] =&gt; php )</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(57)"><span id="btntext57">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(57)"><span id="btntext57">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace57"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer57"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) Array ( [1] =&gt; hello [2] =&gt; hello [3] =&gt; hello [4] =&gt; hello [5] =&gt; php )<br/>Array ( [5] =&gt; php )</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> This code utilizes array_fill() and array_merge().</p><ul class="points"><li>$a1 = array_fill(1, 4, "hello");: Creates an array starting with key 1, filling 4 elements with "hello". $a1 is Array ( [1] =&gt; hello [2] =&gt; hello [3] =&gt; hello [4] =&gt; hello ).</li><li>&amp;bull; $a1 = array_fill(1, 4, &amp;quot;hello&amp;quot;);: Creates an array starting with key 1, filling 4 elements with &amp;quot;hello&amp;quot;. $a1 is Array ( [1] =&amp;gt; hello [2] =&amp;gt; hello [3] =&amp;gt; hello [4] =&amp;gt; hello ).</li><li>$a2 = array_merge($a1, $b1);: Merges $a1 and $b1. Since their numerical keys do not overlap where elements are added, the keys are preserved. $a2 becomes Array ( [1] =&gt; hello [2] =&gt; hello [3] =&gt; hello [4] =&gt; hello [5] =&gt; php ).</li><li>print_r($a2); displays the merged array.</li><li>echo "&lt;br&gt;"; prints a line break.</li><li>print_r($b1); displays $b1, which remains unchanged. The output corresponds to option (C).</li></ul></div><hr/><p><strong>58. What will be the output of the following PHP code?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$names = array("Sam", "Bob", "Jack");
echo $names[0] . "is the brother of " . $names[1] . " and " . $names[1] . ".";
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Sam is the brother of Bob and Jack.</li><li>Sam is the brother of Bob and Bob.</li><li>Sam is the brother of Jack and Bob.</li><li>Error</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(58)"><span id="btntext58">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(58)"><span id="btntext58">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace58"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer58"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) Sam is the brother of Bob and Bob.</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> This is a straightforward array access and string concatenation example.</p><ul class="points"><li>$names[0] is "Sam".</li><li>$names[1] is "Bob".</li><li>$names[2] is "Jack". The echo statement concatenates the literal strings with the values retrieved from the array at the specified indices. Since $names[1] is used twice, "Bob" will appear twice in the output.</li></ul></div><hr/><p><strong>59. Consider the PHP code below. What will be its output?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$names = array("Sam", "Bob", "Jack");
echo $names[0]." is the brother of ".$names[1]." and ".$names[1].".".$brother;
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Sam is the brother of Bob and Bob.$brother</li><li>Sam is the brother of Bob and Bob.</li><li>$brother</li><li>Error</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(59)"><span id="btntext59">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(59)"><span id="btntext59">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace59"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer59"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) Error</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The code attempts to concatenate the undefined variable $brother at the end of the string. In PHP, using an undefined variable in a string concatenation (outside of double quotes for interpolation) will typically result in a Notice being issued by PHP, but the script will continue execution, treating the undefined variable as an empty string.</p><p>However, if error reporting is set to strict levels or if the environment is configured to treat Notices as Fatal Errors, it could lead to a fatal error. Given the options, "Error" is the most appropriate answer as it points to a problem with the code. (A "Notice: Undefined variable" would be generated, and the output would be "Sam is the brother of Bob and Bob."). Assuming a strict interpretation for an MCQ, an "Error" is intended.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>60. What will be the output of the following PHP code?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$place = array("NYC", "LA", "Paris");
array_pop($place);
$place1 = array("Paris");
$place = array_merge($place, $place1);
print_r($place);
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Array ( [0] =&gt; LA [1] =&gt; Paris [2] =&gt; Paris )</li><li>Array ( [0] =&gt; NYC [1] =&gt; LA [2] =&gt; Paris)</li><li>Array ( [0] =&gt; NYC [1] =&gt; LA [2] =&gt; Paris [3] =&gt; Paris )</li><li>Array ( [0] =&gt; LA [1] =&gt; Paris )</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(60)"><span id="btntext60">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(60)"><span id="btntext60">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace60"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer60"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) Array ( [0] =&gt; NYC [1] =&gt; LA [2] =&gt; Paris)</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> Let's trace the array operations:</p><ul class="points"><li>$place = array("NYC", "LA", "Paris");</li><li>array_pop($place);: Removes the last element ("Paris") from $place. $place is now Array ( [0] =&gt; NYC [1] =&gt; LA ). (Numerical keys are re-indexed by array_pop if it's an indexed array).</li><li>$place1 = array("Paris");</li><li>$place = array_merge($place, $place1);: Merges the modified $place with $place1. array_merge re-indexes numerical keys sequentially. $place becomes Array ( [0] =&gt; NYC [1] =&gt; LA [2] =&gt; Paris ).</li><li>print_r($place); displays the final array.</li></ul></div><hr/><p><strong>61. Which of the following is a built-in function in PHP that adds a value to the end of an array?</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>array_push()</li><li>inend_array()</li><li>into_array()</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(61)"><span id="btntext61">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(61)"><span id="btntext61">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace61"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer61"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) array_push()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The array_push() function pushes one or more elements onto the end of an <a href="php-array">array</a>. You can also use the shorthand $array[] = $value; to add a single element to the end of an array.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>62. The function in PHP that can be used to concatenate array elements to form a single delimited string is -</strong></p><ol class="pointsu"><li>implode()</li><li>concat()</li><li>explode()</li><li>concatenate()</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(62)"><span id="btntext62">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(62)"><span id="btntext62">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace62"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer62"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) implode()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> <a href="php-string-implode-function">PHP implode()</a> is a string function that joins the array elements into a string. The implode() function works the same as the join() function and returns a string created from the elements of the array. Basically, this function joins all elements of an array into one string.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>63. Which of the following functions is used to compute the difference between two arrays in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>diff_array</li><li>array_diff</li><li>arrays_diff</li><li>diff_arrays</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(63)"><span id="btntext63">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(63)"><span id="btntext63">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace63"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer63"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) array_diff</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The <a href="php-array-diff-function">array_diff()</a> function compares two or more arrays and returns an array with the keys and values from the first array only if the value is not present in any of the other arrays.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>64. What will be the output of the following program?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"> &lt;?php
$a = array(16, 5, 2);
echo array_product($A);
?&gt;
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>160</li><li>1652</li><li>80</li><li>32</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(64)"><span id="btntext64">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(64)"><span id="btntext64">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace64"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer64"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) 160</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The <a href="php-array-product-function">array_product() function</a> calculates the product of all the values in an array. In this case, 16 * 5 * 2 = 160.</p></div><hr/><h2 class="h2">String Manipulation</h2><p><strong>65. Which of the following is the use of strlen() function in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>The strlen() function returns the type of string</li><li>The strlen() function returns the length of the string</li><li>The strlen() function returns the value of the string</li><li>The strlen() function returns both value and type of string</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(65)"><span id="btntext65">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(65)"><span id="btntext65">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace65"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer65"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) The strlen() function returns the length of string</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The strlen() function is a predefined function of PHP. It is used to find the length of a string or returns the length of a string, including all the whitespaces and special characters.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>66. Which of the following is the use of strpos() function in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>The strpos() function is used to search for the spaces in a string</li><li>The strpos() function is used to search for a number in a string</li><li>The strpos() function is used to search for a character/text in a string</li><li>The strpos() function is used to search for a capitalized character in a string</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(66)"><span id="btntext66">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(66)"><span id="btntext66">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace66"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer66"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) The strpos() function is used to search for a character/text in a string</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The strpos() is an in-built function of PHP. It is used to find the position of the first occurrence of a string inside another string or substring in a string. It is case-sensitive.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>67. Which of the following is the correct use of the strcmp() function in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>The strcmp() function is used to compare the strings excluding case</li><li>The strcmp() function is used to compare the uppercase strings</li><li>The strcmp() function is used to compare the lowercase strings</li><li>The strcmp() function is used to compare the strings, including the case</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(67)"><span id="btntext68">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(67)"><span id="btntext68">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace67"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer67"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) The strcmp() function is used to compare the strings, including the case</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The strcmp() is a string comparison function in PHP. It is a built-in function of PHP, which is case-sensitive, meaning it treats capital and small cases separately. It is used to compare two strings. This function compares two strings and tells whether a string is greater, less, or equal to another string, returning 0, a negative value, or a positive value, respectively.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>68. Which of the following functions is used to get the ASCII value of a character in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>val()</li><li>asc()</li><li>ascii()</li><li>chr()</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(68)"><span id="btntext68">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(68)"><span id="btntext68">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace68"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer68"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) chr()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The PHP chr() function is used to generate a single-byte string from a number. In other words, you can say that it returns a character from a specified ASCII value. (Note: If the question intended to get the ASCII value from a character, the answer would be ord().</p></div><hr/><p><strong>69. Which of the following functions in PHP returns a text in the title case from a variable?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>toUpper($var)</li><li>ucwords($var)</li><li>ucword($var)</li><li>All of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(69)"><span id="btntext69">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(69)"><span id="btntext69">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace69"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer69"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) ucwords($var)</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The ucwords() is an in-built function of PHP, which is used to convert the first character of each word to uppercase in a string. It takes a string as input and converts the first character of each word of the string to uppercase. The other characters of the string remain the same.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>70. Which of the following functions converts a string to all uppercase?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>upper()</li><li>uppercase()</li><li>struppercase()</li><li>strtoupper()</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(70)"><span id="btntext70">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(70)"><span id="btntext70">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace70"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer70"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) strtoupper()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The strtoupper() is one of the most popular functions of PHP, widely used to convert a string to uppercase. It takes a string as a parameter and converts all lowercase English characters of that string to uppercase.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>71. Which PHP function is used to find the position of the last occurrence of a substring inside another string?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>strops()</li><li>strrpos()</li><li>strtr()</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(71)"><span id="btntext71">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(71)"><span id="btntext71">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace71"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer71"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) strrpos()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The strrpos() is an in-built function of PHP which is used to find the position of the last occurrence of a substring inside another string. It is a case-sensitive function of PHP, meaning it treats uppercase and lowercase characters differently.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>72. What will be the output of the following program?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"> &lt;?php
echo ucwords("welcome to the tpoint.com");
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Welcome to the [suspicious link removed]</li><li>welcome to the [suspicious link removed]</li><li>Welcome To The [suspicious link removed]</li><li>Welcome to the [suspicious link removed]</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(72)"><span id="btntext72">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(72)"><span id="btntext72">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace72"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer72"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) Welcome To The [suspicious link removed]</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The `ucwords()` is an in-built function of PHP, which is used to convert the first character of each word to uppercase in a string. It takes a string as input and converts the first character of each word of the string to uppercase. The other characters of the string remain the same.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>73. What will be the output of the following program?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
echo lcfirst("Welcome To The tpointtech.com");
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>welcome To The [suspicious link removed]</li><li>Welcome To The [suspicious link removed]</li><li>Welcome To The [suspicious link removed]</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(73)"><span id="btntext73">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(73)"><span id="btntext73">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace73"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer73"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) welcome To The [suspicious link removed]</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The lcfirst() is an in-built <a href="php-string-functions">PHP string function</a>. It is used to convert the first character of a string to lowercase. In other words, it makes a string's first character lowercase. It returns the converted string.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>74. What will be the output of the following PHP program?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
echo strtr("Walcoma to the tpointtech.com", "au", "ea");
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Welcome to the [suspicious link removed]</li><li>Wulcomu to tha tpointtech.com</li><li>Welceme to the tpeinttech.com</li><li>Walcoma to the teointtech.com</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(74)"><span id="btntext74">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(74)"><span id="btntext74">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace74"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer74"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> c) Welceme to the tpeinttech.com</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The <a href="php-string-strtr-function">strtr()</a> is an in-built function of PHP, which is used to replace a substring inside another string. It provides the facility to change particular characters in a string. The strtr() function translates characters or replaces substrings. It is a case-sensitive function.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>75. What will be the output of the following program?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$var1="Hello World";
echo strrev("$var1");
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>dlroW olleH</li><li>olleH dlroW</li><li>Hello dlroW</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(75)"><span id="btntext75">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(75)"><span id="btntext75">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace75"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer75"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) dlroW olleH</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The strrev() function is a predefined function of PHP. As its name implies, it is used to reverse a string. It is one of the most basic string operations used by programmers and developers.</p></div><hr/><h2 class="h2">File Handling</h2><p><strong>76. What is the use of fopen() function in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>The fopen() function is used to open folders in PHP.</li><li>The fopen() function is used to open a remote server.</li><li>The fopen() function is used to open files in PHP.</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(76)"><span id="btntext76">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(76)"><span id="btntext76">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace76"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer76"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) The fopen() function is used to open files in PHP</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The PHP fopen() function is used to open a file or URL and returns a resource. The fopen() function accepts two arguments: $filename and $mode. The $filename represents the file to be opened, and the $mode represents the file mode, for example, read-only, read-write, write-only etc.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>77. Which of the following is the correct way to open the file "sample.txt" as readable?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>A) fopen("sample.txt", "r");</li><li>B) fopen("sample.txt", "r+");</li><li>C) fopen("sample.txt", "read");</li><li>D) fopen("sample.txt");</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(77)"><span id="btntext77">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(77)"><span id="btntext77">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace5"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer5"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) fopen("sample.txt", "r");</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The fopen() function accepts two arguments: $filename and $mode. The $filename represents the file to be opened, and $mode represents the file mode, for example, read-only, read-write, write-only, etc. The "r" mode opens the file in read-only mode. It places the file pointer at the beginning of the file.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>78. Which of the following functions is used to find files in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>glob()</li><li>fold()</li><li>file()</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(78)"><span id="btntext78">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(78)"><span id="btntext78">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace78"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer78"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) glob()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The glob() function in PHP is used to return an array of filenames or directories that match a specified pattern.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>79. Which PHP function determines the last access time of a file?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>A) filetime()</li><li>B) fileatime()</li><li>C) filectime()</li><li>D) None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(79)"><span id="btntext79">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(79)"><span id="btntext79">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace79"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer79"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (b) fileatime()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The fileatime() function in PHP is used to return the last access time of a file as a UNIX timestamp. On failure, the fileatime() returns false.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>80. Which PHP function is capable of reading a specific number of characters from a file?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>filegets()</li><li>fget()</li><li>fgets()</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(80)"><span id="btntext80">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(80)"><span id="btntext80">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace80"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer80"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) fgets()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The PHP fgets() function is used to read a single line from the file pointer. It reads up to a specified length or until a newline or EOF, whichever comes first.</p></div><hr/><h2 class="h2">Web Specifics (Forms, Sessions, Cookies)</h2><p><strong>81. Which of the following functions is used to set a cookie in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>createcookie()</li><li>makecookie()</li><li>setcookie()</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(81)"><span id="btntext81">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(81)"><span id="btntext81">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace81"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer81"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) setcookie()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> A <a href="php-cookie">PHP cookie</a> is a small piece of information that is stored in the client's browser. It is used to recognize the user. The PHP setcookie() function is used to send a cookie with the HTTP response. Once the cookie is set, you can access it by the $_COOKIE superglobal variable.</p></div><hr/><h2 class="h2">Date &amp; Time Functions</h2><p><strong>82. Which PHP function converts an English text datetime into a Unix timestamp?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>str_to_time()</li><li>strtodate()</li><li>strtotime()</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(82)"><span id="btntext82">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(82)"><span id="btntext82">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace82"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer82"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) strtotime()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> The PHP strtotime() function is used to convert an English text datetime into a Unix timestamp.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>83. Which of the following functions in PHP returns the time of sunrise of a particular day and location?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>date_sunrise()</li><li>date-sunrise()</li><li>sunrise()</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(83)"><span id="btntext83">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(83)"><span id="btntext83">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace83"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer83"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) date_sunrise()</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> This date_sunrise() function is used to get the timing of sunrise of a particular day and location. This function returns the sunrise time of different locations.</p></div><hr/><h2 class="h2">Output &amp; Miscellaneous</h2><p><strong>84. Which of the following is used to display the output in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>echo</li><li>write</li><li>print</li><li>Both (A) and (C)</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(84)"><span id="btntext84">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(84)"><span id="btntext84">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace84"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer84"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (d) Both (A) and (C)</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> In PHP, both echo and print are language constructs. Both echo and print statements can be used with or without parentheses. You can use these statements to output variables or strings. echo is a statement used to display output. print is a statement used as an alternative to echo many times to display output.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>85. Which of the following is the correct way to print "Hello World" in PHP?</strong></p><ol class="pointsa"><li>"Hello World";</li><li>write("Hello World");</li><li>echo "Hello World";</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(85)"><span id="btntext85">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(85)"><span id="btntext85">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace85"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer85"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) echo "Hello World";</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> PHP echo is a language construct, not a function. Therefore, you don't need to use parentheses with it. The correct way to print "Hello World" in PHP is: echo "Hello World";</p></div><hr/><p><strong>86. What will be the output of the following program?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
echo "Welcome" . "to" . "the" . "tpointtech.com";
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Welcome to the [suspicious link removed]</li><li>Welcome, to, the, [suspicious link removed]</li><li>Welcometothe[suspicious link removed]</li><li>Error</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(86)"><span id="btntext86">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(86)"><span id="btntext86">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace86"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer86"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) Welcometothe[suspicious link removed]</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> In the echo statement, the . (dot) operator is used to join strings (concatenation). It doesn't add spaces between the concatenated parts.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>87. What will be the output of the following program?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
echo "Welcome" , "to" , "the" , "tpointtech.com";
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>Welcome to the [suspicious link removed]</li><li>Welcome, to, the, [suspicious link removed]</li><li>Welcometothe[suspicious link removed]</li><li>Error</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(87)"><span id="btntext87">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(87)"><span id="btntext87">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace87"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer87"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (c) Welcometothe[suspicious link removed]</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> You can pass multiple strings separated by a comma (,) in echo. In an echo statement, the , (commA) operator is also used to join strings for output, similar to concatenation, but without creating a single string internally before outputting. It also doesn't add spaces by default.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>88. What will be the output of the following program?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$var1 = "Hello";
$var2 = "World";
echo $var1, $var2;
?&gt; 
</textarea></div><ol class="pointsa"><li>HelloWorld</li><li>Hello, World</li><li>Hello World</li><li>None of the above</li></ol><button class="showanswer" onclick="showhide(88)"><span id="btntext88">Show Answer</span></button><button class="workspace" onclick="showworkspace(88)"><span id="btntext88">Workspace</span></button><div id="workspace88"> </div><div class="testanswer" id="answer88"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) HelloWorld</p><p><strong>Description:</strong> You can pass multiple strings separated by a comma (,) in echo. In an echo statement, the , (commA) operator is also used to join strings for output. It prints them directly next to each other without adding spaces.</p></div><hr/><p><strong>89. What will be the output of the following program?</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">&lt;?php
$var1 = "Hello";
$var2 = "World";
echo "$var1$var2";
